143 ALUMINA.
Cough with involuntary emission of urine in old withered
looking people.

* Relationship. Similar to: Bar. card. (hypochondriasis of
aged; constipation); Bryon. (peevish, irritable; gastric and
abdominal symptoms ; constipation; throbbing headache;
dry cough with vomiting; stitches in chest; dryness of
mucous surfaces; fever, etc.,); Cale. ostr.; Chamom.
(useful as an intermediate remedy); Coniwm (old people;
loss of power of internal recti of eyes) ; Ferrum (chlorosis ;
relaxed abdomen; disgust for meat, etc.); Kerr. jod. (profuse, transparent leucorrheea); Graphit. (chlorosis; skin
rough, chapped, itching; nails; blepharitis, etc.); Ipec.;
Laches. (sad on waking; climaxis); Pulsat. (tearful, peevish; head, etc., better in open air; ozeena; taste lost;
averse to meat; chlorosis; scanty menses; complaints at
puberty; lack of animal heat; soles of feet sore, << walking; toes red, itching, ete.); Plumbum (colic; constipation, ete.); Ruta (loss of power of internal recti of eyes);
Sepia (irritable, tearful; ozeena; scanty menses; puberty ;
prolapsus uteri; inactive rectum; weakness in urinary organs, etc.); Silic.; Sulphur; Zincum {inner canthus ;
granular lids).

Compare with the following in clergyman’s sore-throat: Arg.
nitr., Kali bich., Lycop., ete.

Alumin. follows: Bryon., Laches., Sulphur ; and is followed
by: Bryon.

Alumin. and Bryon. are complementary.

Antidotes to Alumin.: Bryon., Camphor, Chamom., Ipec.

Alumin. antidotes: lead-poisoning, as in painters’ colic ; ailments from lead.

